 ],
 "literal": "If [I] think, [my] heart will indeed burn; without thinking, / if [I] try to turn [it] back, is it not indeed beyond my measure, causing pain? / Sky-touching is of the nature indeed, [my] love; / a noble person is not, the one whom I associated with indeed.",
 "translation": "If I think of him, my heart burns. If, to stop thinking, I try to turn my mind back, it is beyond my strength and only causes pain. My love is as vast as the sky that touches the heavens. But he whom I embraced is not a noble man.",
 "modern": "நினைத்துப் பார்த்தால் என் நெஞ்சம் வேகின்றது. நினைக்காமல் இருக்க என் மனதைத் திருப்ப முயன்றால், அது என் சக்திக்கு அப்பாற்பட்டதாகி என்னை வருத்துகிறது. என் காதல் வானத்தைத் தொடும் அளவுக்குப் பெரியது. ஆனால், நான் நெருங்கிப் பழகிய அவரோ சான்றோர் அல்லர்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the heroine to her confidante in the Neytal (seashore) landscape, expressing her deep grief over her lover's prolonged absence. She describes the agonizing nature of her love: thinking of him burns her heart, yet trying to divert her mind is an impossible task that only brings more pain. She compares her love to the vast sky, highlighting its immense scale. However, she laments that the man she loved and trusted has proven to be ignoble because he has abandoned her to suffer. The contrast between her sky-high love and his lack of virtue intensifies the emotional pain of her separation.",
